SM(248,{"n":"Genesis 22-23","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Genesis 22-23.mp3","d":3883.8,"s":[[0.24,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in Biblical Studies, and is an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[22.88,"So we'll go ahead and get started."],[25.6,"And when we get started, we'll be picking up at the beginning of Genesis chapter 22."],[38.56,"Completely non-controversial story with which I'm sure no one here is familiar."],[47.2,"So before that, um a little bit of brief review."],[52.8,"And it's going to be brief review because basically what we're about to read is the culmination of the story of Abraham."],[61.4,"Um it's not gonna be much longer, and we're gonna make our first."],[68.28,"So we we're in this second part of the book of Genesis that runs from chapter 12 to chapter 50 that we've talked about being the patriarchal narratives that's describing the generations of this family."],[82.28,"We're very close to making our first transition from Abraham to Isaac."],[88.28,"And um so what we're gonna read tonight is really sort of the denouement of Abraham's story that we've been reading so far."],[100.44,"And the key elements in terms of this. culmination are basically um two things"],[110.24,"Uh number one, uh, the promises to Abraham."],[116.24,"And we've talked about how there are these promises on two levels."],[122.16,"So there is a set of promises."],[126.4,"They've been reiterated several times, but there's a set of promises that has to do with Abraham having many descendants."],[135.3,"A whole bunch of descendants and descendants who are going to form multiple nations, as we've talked about, not just one, but multiple nations."],[145.54,"And um that some of those descendants are going to come to possess this swath of land in the Levant in the future."],[156.94,"That's sort of one set of promises."],[160.7,"The second set of promises that we saw had two pieces."],[166.06,"Number one was that in addition to the promise of multiple seed."],[172.16,"Tons and tons of descendants and different nations being descended from him, that there would be one particular descendant."],[180.96,"particular descendant. And in the short term, that one has taken the form of Isaac"],[190.68,"Isaac is not Abraham's only son, technically."],[195.73,"Because there's also Ishmael."],[198.21,"We're going to see there's even going to be a couple of more before we're done."],[203.41,"But Isaac is his, is described as being his only son in the sense that this singularity of his seed, of this one important line of descent."],[218.85,"And that singular line of descent is what is going to bring about ultimately this larger promise."],[226.85,"promise. We talked about how there's the quantitative about the number of his seed"],[233.49,"There was also the qualitative promise that his seed would be like the stars of heaven."],[238.37,"Not just in number, but in quality"],[243.02,"We talked about how the stars of heaven, for any guy growing up in Ur, anyone else in the ancient Near East, those were the gods, those were angelic beings."],[254.93,"And so there is a promise that through a singular descendant of him, what we now call theosis, the transformation of humanity, is going to happen."],[267.87,"And that involves, as we talked about, the reversal of the problems that we saw in Genesis 1 through 11."],[275.65,"Death came to humanity, sin c cameame to to ru rulele over over hum humananityity. demonic powers"],[283.93,"Through the singular sea, those are going to be defeated, and humanity is going to be restored to its original purpose."],[305.93,"This is very common in biblical prophecy."],[309.37,"When a prophecy is given, there is some sort of immediate thing that happens that lets you know for sure that the ultimate fulfillment is going to happen."],[322.73,"So, for example, in uh Luke chapter 2, angel comes and announces to the shepherds that the Messiah has been born."],[337.75,"The Messiah has been born in Bethlehem."],[341.32,"And stars join in singing the praises."],[345.53,"And then the angel says, This will be assigned to you."],[351.29,"Go over here, and you will find a baby wrapped in swaddling clothes, lying in a manger, laying in an animal food trough."],[358.49,"This is something you don't normally see."],[360.78,"A newborn baby in an animal food trough in a cave."],[364.44,"And so this will be a sign to you."],[366.36,"When you go and you see this, kind of odd thing, then you'll know that the prophecy that this is the Messiah."],[375.06,"This is the Christ."],[376.58,"Then you'll know that that's true."],[380.07,"Or in Isaiah chapter 7, Cyro Ephraimite war is going on."],[387.27,"Syro Ephraimite war is poorly named because it's not between the Syrians and the Ephraimites."],[393.19,"It's the Syrians and the Ephraimites attacking Judah."],[398.79,"And the king of Judah is praying and asking God to deliver them because it looks like they're going to lose this war."],[407.03,"And Isaiah says, This will be assigned to you."],[411.03,"This woman will give birth to a son"],[418.09,"And his name will be Emmanuel, which meets God with us."],[422.06,"And before he is old enough to know the difference between good and evil, while he still can't eat solid food while he's still a baby, God will have delivered you from these attacking armies."],[434.74,"Well, how does that prophecy work?"],[436.34,"Well, when you see this baby born, then you'll know, oh."],[442.15,"God is for sure going to deliver us from these invading armies, because look, the baby was born, just like the prophet said."],[449.83,"So in the New Testament, this is what people are referring to when they ask Jesus for a sign."],[456.21,"And so people come up to Jesus all the time."],[460.42,"When he's traveling from place to place, and say, Oh, show us some sign that what you're telling us is true."],[467.06,"Show us some sign, give us a sign, give us a sign."],[469.46,"And he said, Cursed is the generation who asked for a sign."],[473.35,"And why is why is it so horrible to ask for a sign?"],[478.55,"He's healing people, he's casting out the right, like."],[483.07,"How much sign do you want?"],[484.57,"So so them coming and asking him for an additional sign is just a signifier of their doubts."],[494.75,"Of their disbelief, like their active rejection of him."],[500.04,"You know, oh yeah, all that's old hat."],[502.14,"You know, like do something new, you know."],[506.54,"Um yeah. That's that's exactly it."],[510.94,"So this is functioning within the promises to Abraham."],[516.46,"Um that first layer, you're gonna have a bunch of kids, starting with this one kid you're gonna have when you're a hundred and your wife is ninety."],[529.79,"And they're gonna come and they're gonna take possession of this land is the sign that that other promise is going to come true."],[537.15,"In the future"],[543.97,"This is why the Torah concludes where it does."],[548.53,"And Joshua is essentially an epilogue to the Torah."],[553.17,"And about a year from now, when we get to the end of Joshua, we'll see that at the end of Joshua, it says, thus the Israelites took all the land that was promised to their forefather Abraham, and God failed to fulfill none of his promises."],[568.93,"Meaning that level of the promises, that sign level of his descendants taking that land, was fulfilled at the end of the book of Joshua, according to the book of Joshua."],[581.09,"It straightforwardly says that"],[586.47,"The point being, since we can read here and see that God kept that promise, we know that that larger piece, that singular seed, that descendant, who's going to accomplish those other things is coming."],[606.5,"And St. Paul picks up on this in Galatians."],[610.56,"Calls Christ uniquely Abraham's seed."],[614.38,"Sees Jesus as doing those things."],[616.83,"But he's getting that from the Torah."],[619.87,"Okay So we've seen, repeated several times, this structure of these promises that have been given to Abraham."],[623.63,"And the story of Abraham has revolved around these promises"],[630.95,"About God teaching Abraham what these promises mean, reiterating these promises in various ways to bring forth different elements, different aspects of the promises."],[645.31,"And Abraham, as we've seen, unfortunately, when Abraham has fallen into sin, it's been what?"],[652.35,"It's been when he doubted those promises."],[657.39,"When rather than trusting God and remaining faithful to God and trusting God to be faithful to him and keep the promises he had made, Abraham tried to engineer things himself."],[667.71,"Well, there's no way me and Sarah can have a kid"],[670.73,"Under his own devices."],[674.81,"That's crazy. So I will go and have a kid with my female slave."],[681.35,"Or, oh no, I'm in danger going here, I'll lie about who Sarah is to protect myself."],[688.77,"Rather than trusting God to protect me until these promises are fulfilled."],[695.01,"So that's been sort of the tension in his life."],[700.89,"And so now that Isaac has been born, and he's been told, no, your line, this line of this singular line of this seed is going to be reckoned through Isaac."],[712.89,"Isaac, the son, is literally the embodiment of these promises."],[721.37,"He is the sign to Abraham that should conclusively show that this is true."],[727.05,"It should conclusively allow him to now trust and be faithful to God."],[731.5,"Because his 90 year old wife just gave birth to this baby."],[736.46,"Gave birth to this child."],[738.3,"That's not something that happens."],[741.74,"They knew it didn't happen back then."],[743.74,"They weren't dumb."],[745.58,"They understood menopause happens and women can't have babies anymore."],[750.86,"So this should be the badge."],[753.82,"And so this, this tension between the promises, Abraham's call to trust in them, them being embodied in Isaac, that's what now builds up to this climax that we're about to read in chapter 22."],[770.98,"So unless anybody has any questions or comments or excerpts from 19th century evangelistic literature, we'll go ahead and start in Genesis chapter 22, verse 1."],[792.26,"Now it came to pass after these things that God tested Abraham and said to him, Abra Ahambraham, and he said, Here I am."],[800.34,"Okay So this is yet another time, as we as we mentioned last time."],[811.26,"I know we brought it up last time."],[813.47,"We don't have this exhaustive description, obviously, of every moment of Abraham's life."],[820.27,"We sort of pop in on Abraham's life at these various intervals."],[825.15,"And usually most of them are structured around times when God appears to Abraham."],[830.75,"So this is another one of those."],[834.05,"Where God is going to appear to Abraham."],[835.81,"He talks to him and says, Abraham, Abraham."],[838.85,"He says, Here I am."],[841.57,"Now this, and I'm assuming spoil, I'm assuming everyone has read this story, so not spoiling, but part of the issue of this story surrounds this word test."],[855.85,"What we're about to read is a story about God testing Abraham and what that means."],[862.89,"And when I say issue surrounding this story, I mean there's been a lot of ink spilled on this story."],[870.57,"Kierkegaard wrote a whole book just about this story."],[878.25,"Yeah, this is this has been turned into a philosophical problem."],[883.13,"This has been all these different things."],[885.77,"But part of the problem in terms of interpreting what we're about to read has been historically, and this is recent history, not all of Christian history, is how we read that word tested."],[899.67,"How I read that word tested."],[901.51,"That word could actually be translated tempted."],[906.15,"They don't do that in most Bibles, because of course scriptures also say God tempts no one."],[912.15,"But it's the same word."],[914.12,"It's the same word."],[917.24,"So we tend to think of testing in terms of modern testing."],[926.94,"Someone gives me a test."],[928.62,"I don't know if they had, when I was a kid, I'm an old man, but when I was a kid, they had Iowa basics."],[933.02,"The Iowa test of basic learning"],[939.62,"Where once a year we had to spend a couple days filling in the little bubbles, on like the Scantron sheets with a number two pencil."],[949.63,"And then those got run through a machine, and they'd see, you know, where we stood at in terms of our grade level and math and reading, comprehension, and all of these things."],[961.81,"So it was like an assessment."],[964.31,"It was an assessment."],[965.67,"And I know since no child left behind and stuff, there's all kinds of different testing systems."],[971.75,"Okay, different different testing systems in different schools."],[974.49,"That do these assessments."],[975.61,"And of course, you have tests in school in general."],[978.43,"Um those tests, the person giving the test, the people giving the test, uh, in many cases don't know you at all, but they don't know what you know."],[995.64,"In the sense that you go to take the SAT, the person giving it to you doesn't know what your mathematic and verbal abilities are."],[1005.48,"Who's giving you the test?"],[1006.76,"They're giving you the test to find out."],[1009.9,"The test is to discover or to uncover how much you've actually learned, how you're processing it, how you're doing, where your abilities are."],[1021.19,"So this marks a major difference here."],[1023.45,"Because there's not something about Abraham that God doesn't know."],[1030.54,"So God's going to tell Abraham to do something."],[1033.34,"It's not that God doesn't know whether he'll do it or not."],[1039.14,"God knows exactly what's going to happen before this whole thing starts."],[1047.94,"So that's not what testing means here."],[1052.96,"At all. But there's an older use of this verb here that's translated. as test or sometimes as tempt"],[1065.16,"And that is related to, for example, blacksmithing and metallurgy."],[1072.84,"Which is when you're forging metals, you have to test them repeatedly."],[1079.82,"Because impurities can get into them and make them brittle."],[1085.2,"They have to continually be purified, metals have to be folded to make them stronger."],[1091.3,"And so all along the process of forging something, you're continually testing it to see if it needs to be purified more, or more heat needs to be applied. or it needs to be cooled, or right"],[1109.74,"So this is the kind of usage of this verb that's used not just here, but that's used throughout the scriptures for the idea of being tested, or in some cases translated, tempted by God."],[1129.46,"When St. Paul says the testing of our faithfulness develops perseverance, that's the imagery he's using."],[1142.42,"Yeah, that's um this is the kind of testing."],[1145.3,"This is also, this is also, by the way, when Hebrews talks about Christ being tempted."],[1149.62,"This is the same verb that's used"],[1154.38,"That's one of the places where they translate it tempted instead of tested."],[1159.68,"But that makes a big difference."],[1162.8,"Like how you translate it."],[1164.42,"Hebrews is not saying Jesus thought about sinning and just didn't do it."],[1171.56,"It's saying he was tested."],[1175.5,"He was tested and not found wanting ever."],[1179.5,"Israel after the Exodus is going to be tested in the wilderness, and they're going to be found wanting."],[1188.66,"So the use of this word that God is going to test Abraham is part of setting up this story as the climax of the story of Abraham."],[1203.86,"Because God's been doing something with Abraham through his relationship with Abraham, throughout Abraham's life, to bring him and grow him to maturity."],[1217.06,"To bring his faithfulness into fullness."],[1223.47,"Where Abraham messed up, where he sinned, where he did wrong."],[1227.57,"And where God had to explain things to him and correct him and bring him along."],[1232.39,"And so we're coming now toward the end of Abraham's life."],[1239.11,"And so now we get this testing because we're we're getting near to the final product."],[1247.0,"Of Abraham. And so this is giving us a particular view of the story of Abraham we've been reading, his relationship with God, and through that of our relationship with God throughout our life."],[1262.46,"That God is one of the reasons why we call God our Father, is that He's helping raise us and grow us and discipline us and mature us into someone who we don't start out as."],[1278.35,"And bring us along"],[1280.77,"And so we're now we're now reaching this uh this climax."],[1286.21,"So verse 2, Abraham said, Here I am."],[1291.25,"Then he, being God, said, Take now your beloved son Isaac, whom you love, and go to the land of Moriah."],[1300.29,"I said that just for the Lord of the Rings fans."],[1302.45,"It's Moriah. Go to the land of Moriah, no dwarfs, and offer him there as a whole burnt offering on one of the mountains I tell you."],[1314.21,"Okay. So this is the form this test is going to take."],[1320.93,"God comes to him and says, I want you to take your son, who I've told you, your family line is going to be reckoned through."],[1332.23,"All these promises are going to be filled through him."],[1334.63,"I want you to take him."],[1335.75,"I want you to take him to the top of this mountain."],[1339.03,"I want you to sacrifice him to me."],[1341.51,"Burn him up completely."],[1347.59,"So the whether you read Kierkegaard or some other philosophical rendition of this, it focuses on the idea that, well, if you're Abraham, what is the moral thing for you to do if God tells you to go murder your son?"],[1367.45,"That's the focus."],[1369.57,"And there's a reason for this, and that's that once we get into the modern period, everyone psychologizes everything."],[1377.67,"So it's all about I need to get in Abraham's head."],[1380.44,"What would I do if, if God told me to do this?"],[1384.46,"For the record, if you think God is telling you to go and uh sacrifice one of your children by burning them up completely, that's not God."],[1394.22,"I'm just going to lay that out right now."],[1396.4,"So you don't even have to come ask me."],[1399.01,"Um But that's missing the point."],[1403.67,"That's missing the point of the narrative that we're reading here."],[1408.72,"That whole approach to reading the scriptures is bad on any number of counts."],[1414.92,"It's very popular today."],[1417.5,"How did St."],[1418.3,"Peter feel when this and that."],[1419.95,"This psychologizing. It gets even worse when people try to psychologize our Lord Jesus Christ."],[1427.31,"Because of course, he's God."],[1430.13,"So, like, you can't get in his head."],[1435.7,"Like, you don't know what it's like to be God, and you can't, so don't try."],[1441.18,"Um. But even when we're just talking about people."],[1447.2,"That that very modern psychological thing of. I need to try to identify with some character and figure out what they feel"],[1457.48,"That's not the point, of the narrative."],[1461.13,"And you can tell that's not the point of the narrative because we're very rarely told how people feel."],[1466.57,"I mean, once in a while you'll get like so-and-so was sorely grieved."],[1472.09,"That's usually obvious why, because someone they love has died or something."],[1475.79,"But I mean, you don't get this like internal monologue of you know, so-so thought to himself."],[1485.55,"That's not even true of any literature before like the 20th century."],[1490.59,"Like that's what the sources are about."],[1493.23,"yeah, yeah Um."],[1493.35,"Yeah, yeah. That's a that's a very modern literature thing"],[1500.71,"Like if you even if you go and read like early 20th century literature, you go and read Tolkien, you go and read Brian Herbert, you go and read Isaac Asimov, nobody is inside anybody's head."],[1511.59,"Like in any of those any of that literature."],[1514.87,"Um so yeah, we're not here to identify with Abraham."],[1520.33,"We're seeing what is what is God doing with Abraham here."],[1526.21,"Where has Abraham fallen so far in this story?"],[1533.09,"Where is he messed up?"],[1534.77,"But you can't see how God's promise is going to be kept."],[1537.33,"And so he tries to engineer things himself"],[1545.7,"So, if he's still in that place where he was, if this is the old Abraham, if this is Abram who we're dealing with."],[1554.39,"How would he respond to this command?"],[1561.27,"Yeah, exactly. Like, surely this can't be, maybe uh should I go find Ishmael and sacrifice him?"],[1568.69,"Oh my gosh."],[1569.63,"Like, he's my son too."],[1571.75,"Uh. That's what, if this is the way he's previously operated with it, that would be where he goes."],[1582.02,"That's why this is that's why this is the test."],[1586.84,"The test is I tell you to do something that makes no sense."],[1592.54,"I tell you to do something that seems to make it impossible for me to keep the promises I made to you."],[1604.23,"According to your human reasoning."],[1607.83,"Will you trust?"],[1609.83,"Will you be faithful despite that?"],[1613.35,"Or will you fall back into that old pattern?"],[1617.56,"And again, God knows which of those Abraham is going to do."],[1622.8,"But Abraham has to go through this experience of testing to learn Himself."],[1631.54,"Because this is how we learn things in life."],[1635.3,"Especially me. Nobody can tell me much of anything."],[1638.34,"I have to go and do something stupid if I want to learn anything."],[1642.44,"Get hit upside the head with a two by four."],[1645.16,"Then I'm like, oh, okay."],[1648.65,"And so this is this is Abraham is going to go through this experience that God knows he's going to go through."],[1656.7,"That's going to refine that sense of faithfulness."],[1662.0,"And that sense of. trust"],[1671.72,"Verse 3, so Abraham rose early in the morning and saddled his donkey and took two of his young men with him, and Isaac his son, and he split firewood for the whole burnt offering, and arose and went to the place God told him."],[1691.32,"So again, we don't get into Abraham's head."],[1694.68,"We don't have his thought process here."],[1697.58,"Like, oh, what should I do?"],[1699.66,"This is. We're just told, okay, next morning, chop some firewood, get Isaac, get a couple of servants to go with you, because he's over a hundred now."],[1707.66,"He's a very elderly man"],[1712.72,"And let's set off and do this."],[1717.36,"Verse 4. Then on the third day, Abraham lifted his eyes and saw the place afar off."],[1724.0,"Thus Abraham said to his young men, Stay here with the donkey."],[1728.24,"The lad and I will go yonder and worship."],[1730.8,"I love that translation."],[1732.24,"The lad will go yonder and worship, and we will come back to you."],[1738.37,"So this is kind of pragmatic."],[1740.7,"Because if he brings the two servants, they're probably not going to be cool with the whole human sacrifice angle of this."],[1749.31,"So he says, you guys wait here, we'll be right back"],[1756.23,"Notice he says, though, we'll be right back."],[1760.39,"Keep that in the back of your head."],[1762.23,"Does the uh phrase lend his eyes like any significance?"],[1767.99,"Well, it is on the third day, also."],[1772.25,"And he lit he's lifting his eyes because it's on a mountain."],[1777.06,"So on the third day he sees the mountain out ahead of him."],[1787.27,"Verse 6. So Abraham took the firewood of the whole burnt offering and laid it on Isaac his son."],[1791.99,"And the two of them went together"],[1792.51,"Then he took the fire in his hand and a knife."],[1799.31,"So he gets the supplies for the sacrifice."],[1805.39,"And the two of them head out and leave the servants."],[1809.79,"Verse 7. Then Isaac spoke to Abraham his father and said, My father."],[1814.03,"And he said, Here I am, my son."],[1817.79,"He said, Look the fire and the firewood, but where is the sheep for a whole burnt offering?"],[1823.15,"Isaac, not stupid."],[1825.8,"He's like, We got everything except a sacrifice."],[1834.73,"And Abraham said, My son, God will provide for himself the sheep for a whole burnt offering."],[1836.01,"So the two of them went together"],[1843.25,"They came to the place where God had told him, and Abraham built an altar there and placed the firewood in order, and he bound Isaac his son hand in foot and laid him on the altar upon the firewood."],[1859.33,"So something to notice here."],[1862.37,"We did see Isaac say something earlier."],[1865.57,"We don't have a record of Isaac saying anything here."],[1870.77,"And this is something that people are going to note"],[1876.85,"When you look at most Jewish literature from the couple of centuries before Christ and in the first century AD, that talks about the passages in Isaiah that we'll read in a few years when we get to them."],[1899.37,"I said that stuff the first time through, and people laughed, and then we got there."],[1903.13,"So the um uh about the passages in Isaiah that we sometimes call the servant songs, stuff about the suffering servant, the things we often quote are liturgical practice about Christ, like he was led like a sheep to the slaughter, as a spotless lamb is done before his shear, so he openeth not his mouth."],[1927.09,"Those were mostly interpreted as being about Isaac."],[1934.05,"Because Isaac here doesn't protest."],[1939.94,"Isaac here doesn't protest."],[1941.62,"Isaac just apparently allows himself to get tied up and thrown on an altar covered in wood."],[1948.68,"Is willing to let himself be offered as a sacrifice."],[1952.04,"And so then it's through that symbolism of Isaac, who is, remember, this singular seed through whom ultimately deliverance from sin and death and the demonic powers is going to come."],[1967.1,"It's through that that it's connected to the Messiah."],[1971.83,"And by being connected to the Messiah, it is connected to the life and death and resurrection of Christ."],[1981.19,"But we're going to see there's another important piece of that that we'll see here as we go forward."],[1987.75,"But a number of the and there are a number of prayers from that period that are also reflections on this in the Torah."],[1998.49,"That see this moment as Isaac sort of agreeing to be offered to God as a sacrifice, if that's what's necessary."],[2009.05,"And that dynamic then is sort of a subset."],[2015.11,"Because what is Abraham being called to do?"],[2017.19,"What's the point of this test?"],[2019.35,"Abraham is being called to be faithful to God despite that faithfulness."],[2027.35,"What he has to concretely do to exhibit that faithfulness making no sense."],[2035.16,"Yeah. And being horrible."],[2037.78,"And making absolutely no sense."],[2039.86,"Just seeming to be a completely evil act that sabotages all of God's promises to him."],[2045.39,"But to be faithful anyway."],[2048.72,"And so within this, we see this image of Isaac, who at his young age has apparently. already learned this particular lesson"],[2059.32,"That Abraham is now fully experiencing."],[2067.32,"Isaac is an interesting figure."],[2071.0,"Isaac is the only figure in the patriarchal narratives, and one of the only figures in the whole of the Hebrew Bible about whom pretty much nothing negative is really said."],[2090.68,"Like, there's not really."],[2093.48,"I mean, the closest thing to a negative thing is going to be later, he's going to show preference for his firstborn son, which was just sort of normal."],[2108.58,"Right, but that's other people tricking him."],[2111.36,"That's not like a failing of his."],[2113.22,"The way we've seen Abraham has these moral failings."],[2115.86,"Jacob is definitely gonna have moral failings."],[2119.1,"Even Joseph, is gonna be kind of proud and arrogant as a kid."],[2122.74,"So Isaac is sort of in a different category."],[2126.5,"But this particular lesson that his father has spent his life needing to learn, Isaac kind of already has."],[2133.0,"To his father."],[2137.77,"Then Abraham stretched out his hand and took the knife to slay his son"],[2138.29,"Verse 10. But the angel of the Lord called to him from heaven and said, Abraham, Abraham."],[2148.29,"So he said, Here I am."],[2151.17,"Notice how the word spoken by the angel of the Lord and the word spoken by God in verse 1 are identical."],[2162.21,"He then replied, Do not lay your hand on the lad or do anything to him."],[2166.53,"For now I know that you fear God, since for my sake you have not spared your beloved Son."],[2174.53,"Now look at that language."],[2178.53,"Do not lay your hand on the letter, do you there, for now I know that you fear God."],[2184.05,"So that's third person."],[2185.73,"Since for my sake you have not spared your beloved son"],[2193.37,"That's first person."],[2196.81,"So this is one of many places we'll see along the way where the angel of the Lord kind of is God and isn't the same person as God at the same time."],[2214.49,"Which is going to be important."],[2217.13,"But we're here in Genesis already seeing examples of this."],[2221.89,"So what does it mean now I know."],[2226.49,"Does it mean that knowing is half the battle?"],[2231.14,"So this is, again, we could read this as, oh, God really didn't know what Abraham would do."],[2237.38,"But now that Abraham did this, you know, it was like, oh no, he's going through with it."],[2242.34,"Quick, stop him."],[2244.98,"Like. No. So the word here in the Greek that's translated no, uh, to know, is um."],[2255.82,"We'll talk about the Hebrew word too."],[2257.82,"But is a word that's actually related to a verb of seeing."],[2263.18,"There's several different words for knowing in Greek."],[2266.78,"And this one is is one that's see related to seeing."],[2272.14,"And it means to know in the sense that you've had it demonstrated to you."],[2280.28,"So, like if someone, if I went and worked a differential equation on a chalkboard, in front of you, and said, here, watch it, here's the problem, worked it all out."],[2291.74,"You would look at it and say, okay, now I know that."],[2295.38,"And the reason you know that is not, it's different than just, oh, I know the answer to that problem."],[2301.7,"It's I understand."],[2304.36,"I understand it because I've followed you through this process."],[2309.72,"We say a see in that sense alre.ady"],[2312.1,"Yeah, yeah. Oh, I see."],[2313.78,"Oh, I get it."],[2315.28,"Like the idea, the Greek word is chosen to give this idea of it's now that Abraham has displayed something."],[2327.37,"And St. James picks up on this when he refers to this in his epistle."],[2333.06,"He's using this episode in Abraham's life to talk about the relationship between faithfulness and works."],[2341.7,"He says, you show me your faithfulness without works."],[2345.22,"I will show you my faithfulness by what I do."],[2348.18,"That show language."],[2351.96,"And so that here Abraham is the faithfulness that God's been working on with him, he's now displayed it for everyone to see."],[2359.24,"That idea This is more like if I talk about knowing my wife."],[2408.23,"So there are people, lifelong friends, her doctor, who know more data about my wife than I do."],[2421.77,"Like her doctor looks at her blood work."],[2425.41,"And knows all these things about her blood pressure."],[2429.11,"Her exact weight, all these details."],[2431.53,"All these bits of data."],[2434.83,"And I don't have all that data."],[2438.03,"But I know my wife in a different way than her doctor does."],[2445.33,"We have a we have a basic understanding that knowing someone and knowing about someone are two different things."],[2452.7,"Knowing someone and knowing of someone are two different things."],[2456.38,"And the Hebrew verb yeda is pointing to the knowing someone."],[2463.34,"And so this is a relational word."],[2468.4,"Describing the relationship between God and Abraham."],[2471.52,"Remember, Abraham is going to be called God's friend."],[2477.57,"And so this now I know is talking about this new level of connection between God and Abraham."],[2489.45,"And after all, Abraham has just done what?"],[2494.57,"He's been willing to sacrifice his son for God."],[2501.21,"Who's going to sacrifice his son for Abraham?"],[2504.09,"That requires him too to know, because he says like God's gonna take care of it."],[2516.92,"And so this."],[2520.08,"Good segue, thank you, Dave."],[2523.8,"I'll segue to it here."],[2525.48,"So this, when you look at, again, ancient interpretation of this story."],[2530.28,"So this isn't just Jewish stuff extra biblical"],[2531.6,"And this is reflected in the New Testament."],[2537.36,"This is also reflected in the New Testament."],[2541.2,"Reading this story, they're like, he told the servants they'd both be back."],[2549.57,"Uh. What's going on?"],[2554.82,"Ancient interpretation was that this reflected a belief by Abraham that Abraham believed so strongly in the promises of God, who had told him, through Isaac your descendants will be reckoned."],[2567.54,"That he believed God would raise Isaac from the dead."],[2578.32,"So why is that important?"],[2580.72,"Well, because they're reading the story of Abraham."],[2583.84,"And if the problem Abraham's been having is not trusting in those promises and being scared and trying to engineer his way around those promises, what would be the ultimate expression of the other pole?"],[2604.16,"It would be there's nothing I can do to stop God from fulfilling these promises."],[2610.32,"Even killing the Son through whom they're all going to come."],[2613.96,"And setting him on fire and burning him to ashes."],[2617.68,"God will still give me descendants through Isaac."],[2618.56,"Somehow So that's the place that Abraham has come to here at the end of his life."],[2631.49,"Not only do I not have to worry that God won't keep his promises, no one can stop him from keeping them, including me."],[2640.93,"And so he can be completely faithful."],[2643.89,"And this is referred to in Hebrews."],[2646.45,"It says that Abraham was willing to sacrifice his son because he believed in the resurrection."],[2654.37,"And there are actually Jewish traditions surrounding this that he did kill Isaac."],[2660.77,"And Isaac was raised from the dead."],[2662.21,"Pre-Christian Jewish traditions"],[2666.33,"Even though that's not what the text here says."],[2670.1,"That he was received back from the dead."],[2672.1,"Isaac was received back from the dead."],[2674.82,"Which is part of the Isaac Messiah connections that I mentioned earlier that start to get drawn in the first century and in early Christianity."],[2686.1,"Early Jewish Christian circles."],[2697.1,"Then Abraham lifted his eyes and looked, and there behind him, a ram was caught in a thicket by its horns."],[2704.98,"So he brought it for a whole burnt offering in the place. of his son"],[2713.9,"Um and pause there."],[2716.36,"Because some of our friends are going to use this to want to talk about substitutionary atonement."],[2724.52,"Oh, see, look."],[2727.32,"God gives him a ram to offer instead of his son."],[2733.0,"What does that assume?"],[2737.24,"That assumes God really needs somebody to kill something."],[2742.57,"And so if I'm not going to let you kill your kid, we gotta kill something."],[2747.77,"So I guess I'll take this ram."],[2753.25,"Kind of a weird interpretation of this passage on its face."],[2760.9,"What was the primary reason for which sacrifices were offered later on in the Torah?"],[2768.98,"Thanksgiving. On feasts."],[2772.12,"Because remember, sacrifices were about meals normally."],[2774.84,"Whole burnt offering, obviously not, but"],[2778.41,"Thanksgiving. Doesn't Abraham have something to be thankful for here?"],[2786.65,"He has either metaphorically, or if you hold to those other traditions, literally, received his son back from the dead."],[2793.21,"back from the dead. Jacob's going to receive Joseph, too"],[2795.41,"This is going to be a trope in Genesis, by the way."],[2801.31,"He's received his son back from the dead."],[2804.83,"In some sense."],[2806.19,"That's something to give thanks for."],[2809.95,"And God provides him with the animal to do it with."],[2815.12,"Because everything we offer to God really belongs to him already."],[2821.14,"That's why we say in the liturgy, thine own of thine own."],[2824.1,"We offer it to thee."],[2825.54,"Like, we don't actually have any stuff that doesn't already belong to God that we could give him."],[2831.06,"We give him back some of his."],[2833.08,"So that's what's going on here."],[2835.01,"They're offering this animal to give thanks to God."],[2839.15,"Because Abraham has received his son back from the dead."],[2849.71,"Verse 14. Thus Abraham called the name of the place, the Lord has appeared."],[2856.99,"As it is said to this day, in the mountain the Lord was seen."],[2864.67,"Was he? The angel of the Lord."],[2870.03,"The angel of the Lord."],[2872.59,"So the angel of the Lord appeared and spoke to Abraham, and so he named the place."],[2879.71,"Yahweh has appeared"],[2884.23,"And they say in the mountain, Yahweh was seen."],[2890.39,"So it's again, angel of the Lord."],[2894.23,"Is Yahweh not the same person?"],[2899.13,"Remember what we saw at Sodom and Gomorrah?"],[2902.57,"Yahweh was saying they're talking to Abraham, standing next to him, and then he called down fire from Yahweh in heaven."],[2912.02,"We're tracing this through."],[2914.5,"Really, everything you need to know theologically is in the Torah, if you read it correctly."],[2920.06,"But the New Testament helps a lot with reading it correctly."],[2931.38,"So 15, then the angel of the Lord called to Abraham a second time out of heaven."],[2936.1,"So we're still talking about the angel of Yahweh."],[2942.5,"And said, By myself I have sworn, says the Lord, because you did this thing, and for my sake did not spare your beloved son."],[2951.14,"I will certainly bless you, and assuredly multiply your seed as the stars of heaven and as the sand of the seashore, and your seed shall inherit the cities of their enemies."],[2963.38,"In your seed all the nations of the earth shall be blessed, because you obeyed my voice."],[2971.9,"This is another reiteration of the promises."],[2978.19,"And notice again the two pieces."],[2982.19,"Going to multiply your seed, you're going to take possession of the cities."],[2987.56,"In Canaan. And in your seed all the nations of the earth shall be blessed, because you obeyed my voice."],[2999.45,"That's those two sort of trajectories."],[3004.3,"One being the sign of the other."],[3007.74,"So Abraham returned to his servants, and they rose and went together to the. well of oath, and Abraham dwelt at the well of oath"],[3014.42,"Remember, that's where he made the oath with Abimelech last time."],[3022.58,"Verse 20. Now it came to pass after these things that it was told to Abraham, saying, Indeed, Milka also has born sons to your brother Nahor, Huz his firstborn, Buzz his brother, Kemuel, the father of Aram, Chesed, Hazo, Pildash, Jidlaf, and Bethuel."],[3043.3,"All these are great baby names, by the way."],[3046.82,"Be the only one in their class at school."],[3049.78,"And if you have two, naming one Uz and one Buzz."],[3052.66,"I mean. And Bethuul begot Rebecca"],[3060.38,"That's going to be important in a minute."],[3062.14,"Well, not in a minute."],[3063.02,"It's going to be important next time."],[3066.14,"These eight Milkah bore to Nehor, Abraham's brother."],[3070.78,"His concubine, whose name was Reuma, also bore Tiba, Gahem, Thahash, and Maaka."],[3079.42,"Maaka sounds bad, especially if you're around Greeks."],[3083.1,"So I wouldn't name your kid that."],[3085.18,"But uh Thahash."],[3087.52,"That one's wide open."],[3091.84,"Yeah, Buzz, there are some buzzes."],[3093.92,"Buzz Aldrin. They usually have two Zs, though."],[3099.92,"This is one Z."],[3100.24,"one. Buzz with Z"],[3101.32,"And that would be bad for your kid."],[3103.08,"Because then every time your kid, it's like be like, what's your name?"],[3111.0,"Yeah. Oh, there's way worse."],[3112.68,"I've spent my whole life having to say Stephen with a Ph."],[3117.08,"The whole reason I got a doctorate was so that when people asked me if it was Stephen with a Ph, I could say it's Stephen with a PhD."],[3123.48,"That's the only reason."],[3125.56,"I went massively into debt just for that joke."],[3132.36,"Chapter 23, verse 1."],[3137.0,"Now Sarah lived 127 years."],[3141.16,"Then Sarah died in the city of Kiryath Arba, that is Hebron."],[3145.32,"This is one of those places where a place name has been updated"],[3150.24,"Remember back in the introduction of the Torah, I talked about how there are places where they've added names that are post Moses."],[3157.54,"So Kiryath Arba was the original name Hebron."],[3160.18,"We're going to see is what it's going to get called when it becomes the main city of the tribe of Judah, a long time after Abraham."],[3169.38,"In the land of Canaan, and Abraham came to mourn for Sarah and to weep for her."],[3177.62,"Then Abraham stood up from before his dead and spoke to the sons of Het, saying, I am a sojourner and a stranger among you."],[3186.5,"Give me therefore a burial place among you, that I may bury my dead out of my sight."],[3194.38,"So notice this language."],[3198.22,"What was one of the other issues we saw Abraham struggle with earlier in his life?"],[3203.5,"Vis his wife."],[3209.87,"Like how how he how he treated her."],[3212.57,"So it's like, well, I don't want to get killed."],[3215.85,"So I'll let someone else potentially sleep with my wife."],[3222.57,"Not great. Um the way he handled the whole situation with her and Hagar and Ishmael."],[3232.62,"Not great. So this chapter, here's we get to the end of Abraham's life, we're seeing where Abraham ends up."],[3241.96,"We've seen where he was along the way."],[3244.52,"Notice how he keeps describing her."],[3247.32,"My dead. She's dead, but she's still his."],[3256.1,"He's mourning for her, he's weeping for her."],[3260.39,"So this reflects an important attitude, toward a departed loved one."],[3268.33,"First of all, her body isn't some husk that she's done with, and who cares?"],[3272.99,"Oh, you've escaped the prison house of your soul."],[3276.39,"Fly free. This is still her."],[3280.45,"This is the person he loves."],[3283.45,"And his primary concern here is that she be given a proper burial."],[3288.94,"And a proper burial means that she'd be buried in a particular place."],[3293.98,"As we're going to see, a particular place, the location of which is known and recorded here in the Torah."],[3300.7,"Why? So that he and his descendants could go there and visit."],[3312.35,"What does that concretely mean?"],[3314.51,"Ideas, including theological. ideas, come out of practices"],[3322.71,"So if the place where Sarah, the foremother, of the of the later Israelite people, if this is a place that's recorded where not only her immediate family, but her descendants for generations and generations."],[3336.61,"You could go visit it today, so generations and generations, would go and visit."],[3343.81,"What does that mean?"],[3347.41,"That means she's still there."],[3351.49,"Not just still there in the sense that she has it cease to exist, still there in that place."],[3360.37,"Her body, her relics are still connected to her"],[3365.93,"And someone going there, visiting that place is visiting her and has a connection with her that has survived her physical death."],[3381.45,"This is built in here."],[3383.77,"This practice makes no sense otherwise."],[3389.26,"So if anybody comes around telling you that the ancient Israelites didn't believe in an afterlife, you should laugh at them politely."],[3401.62,"Because that's ridiculous."],[3403.9,"They come to you and tell you that they didn't believe in something similar to our idea of saints who pray for people, that they didn't believe in relics, that they didn't believe in pilgrimages."],[3417.42,"Okay. You don't have to laugh."],[3419.42,"You can politely demure and take them here."],[3424.3,"Genesis, first book of the Bible."],[3426.38,"First half of it even."],[3429.66,"First burial. That we see."],[3437.6,"This is part and parcel, not just of Christianity."],[3443.1,"But of biblical religion."],[3445.74,"Going all the way back to Genesis."],[3447.66,"This idea Those other ideas, of the body being the prison house of the soul, of the person going free, some kind of disembodied afterlife, or reincarnation or something, these are things that Greek guys come up with like a thousand years after Moses."],[3473.06,"They have nothing to do with biblical religion."],[3484.7,"The first procession with relics is going to happen in the book of Exodus, spoilers, when they carry Joseph's bones out of Egypt."],[3494.7,"But anyway. So this is important."],[3498.52,"This isn't just like a footnote."],[3505.24,"This is a critically important story, not just in the sense of being the climax of Abraham and Sarah's relationship, but of telling us something about death in the wake of Genesis one through eleven."],[3525.72,"So the sons of Het answered Abraham, saying, Hear us, my lord, you are a king from God among us."],[3533.4,"Bury your dead in the choicest of our burial places"],[3536.72,"None of us will withhold his burial place from you to bury your dead there."],[3542.48,"So what does this mean?"],[3543.44,"You are a king from God."],[3544.48,"Well, remember the whole story with Abimelech."],[3547.22,"And the things that happened."],[3548.26,"But also remember a king."],[3552.34,"At this point in history, circa 2,000 BC, kings are priest kings."],[3570.64,"And were the primary point of connection with God or the gods."],[3575.78,"And in the story with Abimelech from last time, they clearly saw that Abraham had this special kind of relationship with his God."],[3585.46,"They may not know all the ins and outs of that, but they know that much."],[3590.02,"And so they say, hey, you're right."],[3594.98,"The word for a priest king in uh Sumerian is Lugal, which literally means big man."],[3603.94,"You're a big man."],[3606.79,"So none of us, any of us who you ask for a burial place, we're gonna give it to you."],[3616.12,"Then Abraham stood and bowed himself to the people of the land, the sons of Het"],[3621.16,"Thus he spoke with them, saying, If you have it in mind for me to bury my dead out of my sight, then listen to me, and speak on my behalf to Ephron the son of Zohar, that he may give me the cave of Makpalah, which he has at the end of his field, let him give it to me at the full price as a burial place among you."],[3640.96,"So they said, We'll give you, any of us will give you any any place you want."],[3646.48,"He says, I want to pay full price for this one in particular."],[3654.0,"Verse 10. Now Ephron was sitting among the sons of Het, and Ephron the Hittite answered Abraham in the presence of the sons of Het, and all who entered at the gate of the city, saying, Now the gate of the city, remember, they didn't have courts, they didn't have a city council."],[3671.69,"The men of the city, the chief men of the city, would gather at the city gates during the day."],[3679.45,"And there they would decide disputes."],[3682.89,"There they would witness business contracts and that kind of thing."],[3686.17,"So that's why this is happening there."],[3687.61,"This is a real estate contract, essentially."],[3690.43,"And so these sons of Het are there as witnesses to this deal between Ephron and Abraham."],[3702.67,"So he says in the presence of all those in the gate of the city."],[3706.59,"Verse 11: No Know, my Lord, hear me, I give you both the field and the cave within it."],[3711.47,"I give it to you in the presence of all my fellow citizens, bury your dead."],[3716.95,"Then Abraham bowed himself before the people of the land, and he spoke to Ephron in the hearing of the people of the land, saying, If you are for me, hear me, I will give you money for the field."],[3728.63,"Take it from me, and I will bury my dead there."],[3732.31,"Ephron then answered Abraham, saying to him, My Lord, listen to me."],[3736.15,"The land is worth four hundred silver drachmas, but what is that between you and me?"],[3741.11,"So bury your dead."],[3742.47,"So this is the first instance of anti-haggling."],[3747.21,"Your money's no good here."],[3748.57,"Take my money."],[3751.85,"Verse 16. So Abraham listened to Ephron, and Abraham weighed out the silver for Ephron. which he had mentioned in the hearing of the sons of Het, the 400 silver dragmas, currency of the merchants"],[3764.05,"Thus the field of Ephron in Mokpalah, which was opposite Mamre, the field and the cave in it, and all the trees in the field, which were within all the surrounding borders, were deeded to Abraham as his possession, in the presence of the sons of Het before all who entered the city."],[3780.05,"Then after this, Abraham buried Sarah, his wife, in the cave of the field of Mokpalah, which was opposite Mamre, that is Hemr Hebron."],[3788.37,"Again, that's an update."],[3790.69,"In the land of Canaan."],[3792.37,"So the field and the cave in it were deeded to Abraham by the sons of Het as property for a burial place."],[3799.57,"And this place, again, not only we're going to see it turn up again, but not only is this burial place there throughout the Old Testament period, you can go there today."],[3812.49,"This is a known spot and pilgrimage site."],[3818.33,"And today it's a pilgrimage site for everybody."],[3821.29,"That's not only do uh Jewish people go there, but Christians and Muslims, even."],[3830.01,"Because Abraham. To this burial site."],[3833.34,"Um, this is a known site."],[3838.1,"So uh we won't get into uh chapter 24, because as you can see, that's a longer chapter, and we're going to be getting into the story of Isaac."],[3846.18,"So we'll finish here, unless anybody has any questions"],[3851.26,"We'll finish here at uh the end of Abraham's story."],[3855.82,"Get ready to get into Isaac next time."],[3857.58,"So thanks everybody."],[3861.42,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[3868.78,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancient faith.com."],[3874.14,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith dot com"]],"o":[{"t":22.88,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: culmination of Abraham's story","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at the beginning of <b>Genesis 22</b> - \"completely non-controversial story\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Brief review: what we read is the <b>culmination</b> of Abraham's story</li><li class=\"d0\">Second part of Genesis: <b>chs 12-50</b>, <b>patriarchal narratives</b>, generations of this family</li><li class=\"d0\">Very close to first transition: <b>Abraham → Isaac</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Tonight = <b>denouement</b> of Abraham's story</li><li class=\"d0\">Two key elements: the <b>promises</b>, and Abraham's trust in them</li></ul>"},{"t":110.24,"lv":3,"h":"The promises on two levels","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Level one</b>: many descendants</li><li class=\"d1\">a whole bunch, forming <b>multiple nations</b>, not just one</li><li class=\"d1\">some will come to possess a <b>swath of land in the Levant</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Level two</b>: one particular descendant</li><li class=\"d1\">short term: <b>Isaac</b> - not Abraham's only son technically (<b>Ishmael</b>, and a couple more before we're done)</li><li class=\"d1\">Isaac = \"only son\" in sense of the <b>singular line of descent</b></li><li class=\"d1\">that singular line brings about the larger promise</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Quantitative</b> (number of seed) and <b>qualitative</b>: seed like the <b>stars of heaven</b></li><li class=\"d1\">stars = <b>gods, angelic beings</b> to anyone growing up in <b>Ur</b>/ancient Near East</li><li class=\"d1\">through a singular descendant: <i>theosis</i> = <b>transformation of humanity</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>reversal of Genesis 1-11</b>: death, sin ruling, demonic powers <b>defeated</b>; humanity restored to original purpose</li></ul>"},{"t":305.93,"lv":2,"h":"Prophecy and signs","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Common in prophecy: an <b>immediate thing</b> happens → confirms the <b>ultimate fulfillment</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Luke 2</b>: angel tells shepherds Messiah born in Bethlehem</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sign</b>: baby in swaddling clothes in an <b>animal food trough</b>, in a cave - \"you don't normally see\"</li><li class=\"d1\">see the odd thing → know the prophecy true</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Isaiah 7</b>: <b>Syro-Ephraimite war</b> - Syrians and Ephraimites attacking <b>Judah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">king of Judah praying for deliverance</li><li class=\"d1\">sign: woman gives birth to a son, <b>Emmanuel</b> = God with us</li><li class=\"d1\">before he knows good from evil, still a baby, God will have <b>delivered</b> you</li><li class=\"d1\">see the baby born → <b>certain</b> God will deliver from armies</li><li class=\"d0\">NT: people asking Jesus for <b>a sign</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"cursed is the generation who asks for a sign\" - healing, casting out demons: \"how much sign do you want?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">asking = <b>doubt</b>, active rejection: \"do something new\"</li></ul>"},{"t":510.94,"lv":3,"h":"Signs inside the promises to Abraham","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Level one = the <b>sign</b>: kids starting with one at <b>100</b> (wife <b>90</b>), possessing the land</li><li class=\"d1\">sign that the other promise will come true</li><li class=\"d0\">Why the Torah concludes where it does: <b>Joshua</b> = <b>epilogue</b> to the Torah</li><li class=\"d1\">end of Joshua (\"a year from now\"): Israelites took all the land promised to Abraham, God <b>failed in none</b> of his promises</li><li class=\"d1\">sign level fulfilled per Joshua</li><li class=\"d0\">Since God kept that, the <b>larger piece</b> - singular seed - is coming</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. Paul in Galatians</b>: Christ uniquely <b>Abraham's seed</b> - but getting it from the Torah</li></ul>"},{"t":619.87,"lv":2,"h":"Abraham's pattern: doubting the promises","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Abraham's story revolves around the promises</li><li class=\"d1\">God teaching what they mean, reiterating to bring out aspects</li><li class=\"d0\">Abraham's <b>sin = doubting</b> the promises</li><li class=\"d1\">instead of trusting, <b>engineered things himself</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"no way me and Sarah can have a kid\" → child with <b>female slave</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"I'm in danger\" → <b>lie about Sarah</b> to protect himself</li><li class=\"d0\">Tension of his life</li></ul>"},{"t":700.89,"lv":3,"h":"Isaac as embodiment of the promises","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Now Isaac born; line reckoned through him</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Isaac = embodiment</b> of the promises</li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>sign</b> that should conclusively show it's true</li><li class=\"d1\">should allow Abraham to <b>trust and be faithful</b>: 90-year-old wife gave birth</li><li class=\"d1\">they knew <b>menopause</b> - \"they weren't dumb\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Tension: promises vs Abraham's call to trust, embodied in Isaac → builds to the <b>climax of ch 22</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Unless anyone has comments or excerpts from 19th century evangelistic literature…\"</li></ul>"},{"t":770.98,"lv":2,"h":"Genesis 22:1 - God tested Abraham","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>22:1</b>: after these things <b>God tested Abraham</b>: \"Abraham, Abraham\" - <b>\"Here I am\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Genesis = pop-ins on Abraham's life at intervals</li><li class=\"d1\">most structured around <b>God appearing</b> to him; another one</li><li class=\"d0\">Issue: the word <b>test</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Kierkegaard</b> wrote a whole book; turned into a <b>philosophical problem</b></li><li class=\"d1\">word could be translated <b>tempted</b> - same word; most Bibles don't because \"God tempts no one\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Modern testing: <b>Iowa Basics</b>, SAT - <b>assessment</b></li><li class=\"d1\">tester doesn't know what you know; test <b>uncovers</b> it</li><li class=\"d0\">Here: nothing about Abraham God doesn't know</li><li class=\"d1\">God knows <b>exactly what will happen</b> before it starts → <b>not</b> that kind of testing</li></ul>"},{"t":1052.96,"lv":3,"h":"Testing as metallurgy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Older use of the verb: <b>blacksmithing, metallurgy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">forging metals: <b>test repeatedly</b> - impurities make them <b>brittle</b></li><li class=\"d1\">purify, <b>fold</b> to strengthen; heat, cool</li><li class=\"d0\">Same usage throughout scripture for being tested/tempted by God</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>St. Paul</b>: testing of faithfulness develops <b>perseverance</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Hebrews</b>: Christ <b>tempted</b> - same verb</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"thought about sinning and didn't\" → <b>tested and never found wanting</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Israel</b> after Exodus: tested in wilderness, <b>found wanting</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Sets this up as the <b>climax</b> of Abraham's story</li><li class=\"d1\">God has been growing him to <b>maturity</b>, faithfulness to <b>fullness</b> - correcting where he sinned</li><li class=\"d1\">near the end of his life → <b>final product</b></li><li class=\"d1\">our relationship with God: why we call God <b>Father</b> - raises, disciplines, matures us into who we <b>don't start out as</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1286.21,"lv":2,"h":"22:2 - Take your beloved son","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>22:2</b>: \"Take now your <b>beloved son Isaac</b>, whom you love\" - land of <b>Moriah</b> (\"no dwarfs\")</li><li class=\"d1\">offer him as a <b>whole burnt offering</b> on one of the mountains I tell you</li><li class=\"d0\">The form of the test</li><li class=\"d1\">the son through whom the family line and <b>all the promises</b> are to be fulfilled</li><li class=\"d1\">top of the mountain, sacrifice him, <b>burn him up completely</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1347.59,"lv":3,"h":"Against psychologizing the text","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Kierkegaard-type reading: what's the <b>moral</b> thing if God tells you to murder your son?</li><li class=\"d1\">modern period: everyone <b>psychologizes</b> everything - \"get in Abraham's head\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"if you think God is telling you to <b>burn your child</b>, that's not God\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>missing the point</b> of the narrative; \"bad on any number of counts\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Worse when people psychologize <b>Christ</b> - \"you don't know what it's like to be God\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Narrative doesn't tell how people feel</li><li class=\"d1\">rarely told; no <b>internal monologue</b> in literature before the <b>20th century</b></li><li class=\"d1\">even early 20th c. (<b>Tolkien</b>, <b>Asimov</b>): nobody inside anybody's head</li><li class=\"d0\">We're not here to <b>identify</b> with Abraham → <b>what is God doing</b> with Abraham?</li></ul>"},{"t":1545.7,"lv":3,"h":"Why this is the test","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Old <b>Abram</b> would have <b>engineered</b> it</li><li class=\"d1\">\"maybe I should go find <b>Ishmael</b> and sacrifice him\" - he's my son too</li><li class=\"d0\">The test: I tell you to do something that makes <b>no sense</b></li><li class=\"d1\">seems to make it <b>impossible</b> for me to keep my promises, by <b>human reasoning</b></li><li class=\"d1\">will you <b>trust</b>, be faithful - or fall back into the old pattern?</li><li class=\"d0\">God knows which; <b>Abraham has to learn it himself</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"nobody can tell me anything; I have to do something stupid\" - <b>two by four</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Experience <b>refines</b> faithfulness and trust</li></ul>"},{"t":1671.72,"lv":2,"h":"22:3-5 - Rising early, the third day","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>22:3</b>: <b>rose early</b>, saddled <b>donkey</b>, two young men + Isaac, split firewood</li><li class=\"d1\">again <b>no look into Abraham's head</b> - just: chop wood, get Isaac, servants (he's over 100)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>22:4</b>: <b>third day</b> - lifted his eyes, saw the place <b>afar off</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Q: significance of \"lifted his eyes\"? - third day; lifting eyes because it's a <b>mountain</b> ahead</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>22:5</b>: \"Stay here with the donkey. The lad and I will go yonder and <b>worship</b>, and <b>we will come back</b> to you\"</li><li class=\"d1\">pragmatic: servants wouldn't be cool with <b>human sacrifice</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>\"We'll be right back\"</b> - keep in back of your head</li></ul>"},{"t":1787.27,"lv":2,"h":"22:6-8 - Where is the sheep?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>22:6</b>: firewood laid on <b>Isaac</b>; Abraham takes <b>fire</b> and <b>knife</b>; \"the two of them went together\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>22:7</b>: Isaac: \"My father\" - \"Here I am, my son\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Look, the fire and the wood, but <b>where is the sheep</b>?\" - Isaac \"not stupid\": everything except a sacrifice</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>22:8</b>: \"<b>God will provide for himself the sheep</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"the two of them went together\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1843.25,"lv":2,"h":"22:9 - Isaac bound on the altar","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>22:9</b>: altar built, wood in order, <b>bound Isaac hand and foot</b>, laid on the altar</li><li class=\"d0\">Notice: no record of <b>Isaac saying anything</b> here (he spoke earlier)</li></ul>"},{"t":1876.85,"lv":3,"h":"Isaac and the servant songs","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Jewish literature, couple centuries BC and first century AD, on the <b>servant songs</b> of <b>Isaiah</b> - suffering servant</li><li class=\"d1\">\"led like a sheep to the slaughter\", \"as a spotless lamb before its shearer, so he openeth not his mouth\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>mostly interpreted as about Isaac</b> - he <b>doesn't protest</b></li><li class=\"d1\">allows himself to be tied up and thrown on an altar; <b>willing</b> to be offered</li><li class=\"d0\">Through Isaac, the <b>singular seed</b> → connected to the <b>Messiah</b> → to Christ's <b>life, death, resurrection</b></li><li class=\"d1\">another important piece coming</li><li class=\"d0\">Prayers from that period: Isaac <b>agreeing to be offered</b> if necessary</li><li class=\"d0\">Abraham's call: faithful though the concrete act <b>makes no sense</b>, seems <b>evil</b>, sabotages God's promises</li><li class=\"d1\">Isaac, at his young age, has <b>already learned</b> the lesson Abraham is just experiencing</li></ul>"},{"t":2067.32,"lv":3,"h":"Isaac: nothing negative said","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Isaac</b>: only figure in the patriarchal narratives - one of the only in the Hebrew Bible - with <b>nothing negative</b> said</li><li class=\"d1\">closest: later favoring his <b>firstborn</b> - normal, and he's <b>tricked</b> by others, not his failing</li><li class=\"d1\">Abraham: <b>moral failings</b>; Jacob: definitely; even <b>Joseph</b> proud and arrogant as a kid</li><li class=\"d0\">Isaac in a different category - already has his father's lesson</li></ul>"},{"t":2138.29,"lv":2,"h":"22:10-12 - The angel stops him","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>22:10</b>: Abraham stretched out his hand, took the <b>knife to slay</b> his son</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>22:11</b>: <b>angel of the Lord</b> from heaven: \"Abraham, Abraham\" - \"Here I am\"</li><li class=\"d1\">same word as spoken by <b>God in verse 1</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>22:12</b>: \"Do not lay your hand on the lad… now I know you fear God, since <b>for my sake</b> you have not spared your beloved son\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>third person</b> (\"fear God\") then <b>first person</b> (\"for my sake\")</li></ul>"},{"t":2196.81,"lv":3,"h":"The angel of the Lord is God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Angel of the Lord = <b>God and not the same person</b> at once - many examples ahead</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>important</b>; already seen in Genesis</li></ul>"},{"t":2221.89,"lv":3,"h":"\"Now I know\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Misreading: God didn't know → \"oh no he's going through with it, quick, stop him\" - <b>No</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Greek</b> word for \"know\" related to a verb of <b>seeing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">know = <b>demonstrated to you</b>: watch a differential equation worked on a chalkboard → \"now I know\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"oh, I see\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. James</b> picks this up: faithfulness and <b>works</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"show me your faithfulness without works; I'll <b>show</b> you by what I do\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Abraham has now <b>displayed</b> the faithfulness God worked on for everyone to see</li></ul>"},{"t":2359.24,"lv":3,"h":"Hebrew <i>yada</i>: knowing someone","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Knowing my <b>wife</b>: her <b>doctor</b> knows more data (blood work, pressure, weight) - I know her <b>differently</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>knowing someone</b> vs <b>knowing about/of</b> someone</li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew <i>yada</i> = <b>knowing someone</b>: a <b>relational</b> word</li><li class=\"d1\">Abraham to be called <b>God's friend</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"now I know\" = <b>new level of connection</b> between God and Abraham</li><li class=\"d1\">Abraham was willing to sacrifice his son for God - \"who's going to sacrifice his son for Abraham?\"; God will take care of it</li></ul>"},{"t":2525.48,"lv":2,"h":"\"We will come back\": resurrection","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ancient interpretation (not just Jewish extra-biblical; <b>reflected in the NT</b>): he told the servants they'd <b>both</b> be back</li><li class=\"d1\">Abraham believed so strongly in the promises (\"through Isaac your descendants\")</li><li class=\"d1\">→ believed God would <b>raise Isaac from the dead</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ultimate expression of the other pole: <b>nothing I can do stops God</b> fulfilling the promises</li><li class=\"d1\">even killing the son and <b>burning him to ashes</b> - God will still give descendants through Isaac</li><li class=\"d0\">Where Abraham arrives at end of life: <b>no one can stop God keeping his promises, including me</b> → completely faithful</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Hebrews</b>: willing to sacrifice because he believed in the <b>resurrection</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Pre-Christian Jewish traditions</b>: he did kill Isaac, Isaac <b>raised</b> - not what the text says</li><li class=\"d1\">Isaac \"received back from the dead\" → part of the <b>Isaac-Messiah</b> connections, 1st c. and early Jewish Christian circles</li></ul>"},{"t":2697.1,"lv":2,"h":"22:13 - The ram","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>22:13</b>: Abraham lifts eyes: <b>ram caught in a thicket by its horns</b></li><li class=\"d1\">offered as a whole burnt offering <b>in place of his son</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2713.9,"lv":3,"h":"Not substitutionary atonement","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Some of our friends\" use this for <b>substitutionary atonement</b> - \"God gives a ram instead of his son\"</li><li class=\"d1\">assumes God <b>needs somebody to kill something</b> - \"kind of a weird interpretation\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Main reason for later sacrifices: <b>thanksgiving</b>, feasts - sacrifices were about <b>meals</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Abraham has something to be thankful for: received his son <b>back from the dead</b> (metaphorically, or literally per those traditions)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>trope in Genesis</b>: Jacob will receive Joseph too</li><li class=\"d0\">God provides the animal - everything we offer already <b>belongs to him</b></li><li class=\"d1\">liturgy: <b>\"Thine own of thine own\"</b> - we give back some of his</li></ul>"},{"t":2849.71,"lv":2,"h":"22:14-19 - Yahweh seen; the oath","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>22:14</b>: Abraham names the place <b>\"the Lord has appeared\"</b>; \"in the mountain the Lord was seen\"</li><li class=\"d1\">angel of the Lord appeared, yet <b>Yahweh</b> was seen → same person?</li><li class=\"d1\">cf. <b>Sodom and Gomorrah</b>: Yahweh by Abraham, calls down fire from Yahweh in heaven</li><li class=\"d1\">\"everything you need theologically is in the Torah, if you read it correctly - NT helps\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>22:15-18</b>: angel calls a <b>second time</b>: \"<b>by myself I have sworn</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">because you did this and did not spare your beloved son: <b>bless</b>, multiply seed as <b>stars and sand</b>, seed inherits <b>cities of enemies</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>\"In your seed all the nations of the earth shall be blessed\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">another reiteration: <b>two pieces</b> - land, and blessing to the nations - one the <b>sign of the other</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>22:19</b>: back to servants, to <b>Beersheba</b> (well of oath) - where he swore with Abimelech</li></ul>"},{"t":3022.58,"lv":2,"h":"22:20-24 - Nahor's family","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">News: <b>Milcah</b> bore sons to <b>Nahor</b>: <b>Uz</b>, <b>Buz</b>, <b>Kemuel</b>, <b>Chesed</b>, <b>Hazo</b>, <b>Pildash</b>, <b>Jidlaph</b>, <b>Bethuel</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Bethuel begot Rebekah</b> - important <b>next time</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"great baby names\" banter (Uz and Buzz; Buzz Aldrin)</li><li class=\"d0\">Concubine <b>Reumah</b> bore <b>Tebah, Gaham, Tahash, Maacah</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Aside: \"Stephen with a Ph\" → doctorate so he could say \"Stephen with a <b>PhD</b>\" - \"massively into debt for that joke\"</li></ul>"},{"t":3132.36,"lv":2,"h":"Genesis 23:1-2 - Sarah dies","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>23:1</b>: Sarah lived <b>127</b> years; died in <b>Kiriath Arba</b> = <b>Hebron</b></li><li class=\"d1\">another <b>updated place name</b>, post-Moses (see intro to the Torah)</li><li class=\"d1\">Kiriath Arba = original; Hebron = later, main city of tribe of <b>Judah</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Abraham comes to <b>mourn and weep</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>23:3-4</b>: speaks to the <b>sons of Heth</b>: \"I am a <b>sojourner and stranger</b>… give me a <b>burial place</b> to bury my dead out of my sight\"</li></ul>"},{"t":3194.38,"lv":3,"h":"\"My dead\": burial, relics, afterlife","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Abraham's earlier failures with his wife: let someone else <b>potentially sleep with her</b>; Hagar and Ishmael - \"not great\"</li><li class=\"d0\">End of his life: <b>\"my dead\"</b> - she's dead but <b>still his</b>; mourning, weeping</li><li class=\"d0\">Attitude toward the departed</li><li class=\"d1\">body isn't a <b>husk</b>, \"escaped the prison house of your soul\" - <b>still her</b></li><li class=\"d1\">concern: <b>proper burial</b> in a <b>known place</b>, recorded in the Torah → so he and descendants can <b>visit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ideas, including theological ones, come out of <b>practices</b></li><li class=\"d1\">generations visiting → <b>she's still there</b>; body, <b>relics</b> still connected to her; visiting = <b>connection that survived physical death</b></li><li class=\"d1\">practice makes no sense otherwise</li><li class=\"d0\">Anyone saying Israelites had <b>no afterlife</b>, nothing like <b>saints</b> who pray, <b>relics</b>, <b>pilgrimages</b> → \"politely demur\", point to <b>Genesis</b>, first burial</li></ul>"},{"t":3424.3,"lv":3,"h":"Biblical religion vs Greek ideas","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Part and parcel of <b>biblical religion</b>, back to Genesis</li><li class=\"d1\">body as <b>prison house of the soul</b>, disembodied afterlife, <b>reincarnation</b> = \"Greek guys\" ~<b>1000 years after Moses</b>; nothing to do with biblical religion</li><li class=\"d0\">First procession with relics: <b>Exodus</b> - carrying <b>Joseph's bones</b> out of Egypt</li><li class=\"d0\">Not a footnote: climax of Abraham and Sarah's relationship + tells us about <b>death after Genesis 1-11</b></li></ul>"},{"t":3525.72,"lv":2,"h":"23:5-9 - Sons of Heth: \"a king from God\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>23:6</b>: \"you are a <b>king from God</b> among us\" - bury in the <b>choicest</b> tomb</li><li class=\"d1\">recall <b>Abimelech</b> episode</li><li class=\"d1\">circa <b>2000 BC</b>: kings are <b>priest-kings</b>, primary point of connection with God/gods</li><li class=\"d1\">they saw Abraham's special relationship with his God</li><li class=\"d1\">Sumerian <i>lugal</i> = <b>big man</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>23:7-9</b>: Abraham bows; asks them to speak to <b>Ephron son of Zohar</b> for the <b>cave of Machpelah</b> at the end of his field</li><li class=\"d1\">\"at the <b>full price</b>\" - they offer any place; he wants this one</li></ul>"},{"t":3654.0,"lv":2,"h":"23:10-16 - Bargaining at the city gate","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>The gate</b>: no courts or city council - <b>chief men gathered at the gates</b></li><li class=\"d1\">decided disputes, <b>witnessed business contracts</b> → a <b>real estate contract</b>; sons of Heth = witnesses</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>23:11</b>: Ephron: \"I give you both the field and the cave\" - before fellow citizens</li><li class=\"d0\">Abraham insists: \"I will give you <b>money</b> for the field\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>23:15</b>: \"worth <b>400 silver</b> drachmas, but what is that between you and me?\" - first <b>anti-haggling</b></li></ul>"},{"t":3751.85,"lv":2,"h":"23:16-20 - Sarah buried at Machpelah","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>23:16</b>: Abraham <b>weighed out 400</b> silver, \"currency of the merchants\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>23:17-18</b>: field, cave, trees, borders <b>deeded</b> to Abraham before all who entered the gate</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>23:19</b>: buried Sarah in the cave opposite <b>Mamre</b> = Hebron (another <b>update</b>)</li><li class=\"d0\">Known spot, throughout the OT period - <b>you can go there today</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>pilgrimage site</b> for everybody: Jewish, Christian, even <b>Muslims</b> - because of Abraham</li><li class=\"d0\">Not getting into <b>ch 24</b> - longer chapter, the story of <b>Isaac</b></li><li class=\"d0\">End of Abraham's story; <b>Isaac next time</b></li></ul>"}],"next":"Next week: <b>Genesis 24</b> - the story of <b>Isaac</b>"});
